  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "JPY and KRW charges now go to the gateway in the right amount: a ¥1,500 invoice produces `amount: 1500`, not 150,000. `npm test` passes (8 of 8).\n\n**Root cause:** `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lowercases the currency (`jpy`) for the gateway, then passes that lowercase code to `toMinor`. The currency registry in `src/lib/currency/registry.js` only had upper-case keys, so the lookup for `jpy` missed. It fell back to the default of 2 decimal places and multiplied the yen amount by 100. USD and EUR use 2 decimals anyway, so the wrong fallback happened to give the right answer for them, which is why only Japan and Korea were hit.\n\n**Fix:** the registry lookups (`exponent`, `isSupported`, `symbol`) now ignore case. I fixed it there rather than in `payload.js` because `src/payouts/batch.js` and `src/coupons/apply.js` call `toMinor` with a currency code too. If any of them ever received a lowercase code, they would have hit the same bug.\n\n**Tests:** I added two to `test/charge.test.js`. One checks that a JPY and a KRW invoice are charged in whole units. The other checks that `toMinor` handles lowercase codes, including a 3-decimal one (`kwd`).\n\n**Still open:** a currency code the registry doesn't list still silently gets 2 decimals. Throwing an error instead would be safer for money, but it changes behaviour beyond this bug, so I left it.\n\nCustomers who were already overcharged will need refunds. The code fix doesn't touch those charges.",
